+
+The actuator force models of Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles} predict the force a BPA delivers, and the robot knee turns that force into joint torque through mounting brackets, artificial tendons, and paths that wrap over the joint, each of which departs from the rigid-body idealization. That idealization fails in two ways: measured muscle lengths were consistently shorter than predicted, and measured torque deviated from prediction in a load-dependent manner. This chapter formulates the correction terms that absorb these effects (a constant length offset, series compliance, and a wrapping-length loss), each identified from experiment rather than derived analytically; measures isometric torque with \qty{10}{\mm} and \qty{20}{\mm} BPAs on pinned-joint and biomimetic knees; examines the physical sources of the remaining prediction error; re-identifies the correction terms over the full test sets; and redesigns the actuator routes under the corrected model so that the robot meets or exceeds human-scaled torque capability, closing on the transmission-design implications of mono- and bi-pulley tendon routing.
 
